<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Hakam I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 770–822</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Al-Hakam I of Córdoba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Hakam I was the Umayyad emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> from 796 to 822. His reign was marked by harsh repression of internal opposition, including a massacre of notables at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 797 and the crushing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-arrabal">Revolt of the Arrabal</a> in Córdoba in 818, after which the rebellious suburb was razed and its inhabitants expelled. He strengthened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>'s army with professional and slave troops.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-hakam-i.jpg" alt="Al-Hakam I" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Numismática Pliego · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dirham_al_hakam_i_17521.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
